0.1.0
---
Feature
- The library opens, with the family's five scales and no control yet. The
  vocabulary its controls will share, and where it differs from the families
  beside it:

    * the corners are large enough to be the shape rather than a softened edge:
      a control is eight, a card sixteen, a layer over the page twenty-four;
    * the ramp carries weight twice - every step is set at a medium weight
      because text here leans forward, and the strong cut is the step above that
      at the same size. No typeface is shipped with it: the family's own is a
      file, and carrying one would put a licence and a dozen megabytes into
      every application that draws a switch;
    * a surface stands up rather than being lit - the spread is tighter and the
      darkness higher than in a family that draws a lift as light. That is a
      choice, not an approximation: the same lift drawn two ways;
    * the motion has a curve the other families do not: the springy one arrives
      with a little more than it needs and settles back, and it belongs to the
      things that land rather than to anything that merely changes colour;
    * the semantic colours are the system's, in both of the two cuts they come
      in. They are here rather than on the palette because they are not the
      theme's to choose - let the brand's blue follow a theme's accent and it is
      a different design language wearing these shapes.

  What it does not carry is the other half of the likeness: the platform's own
  materials. Those cannot be verified on a machine that does not run the
  platform, so they are not attempted, and a shape that is close without them is
  the goal rather than a shortfall to be filed later.
- `HarmonySwitch`, a switch whose knob answers the hand rather than the
  distance. Qt has a check box and no switch, and the two are not the same
  control: this one says that what it stands for is in force now. The outline -
  a capsule with a knob in it - is what every family draws, so what makes the
  drawing this family's is what the knob does. Held, it grows into a rounded
  rectangle of the family's medium corner, one state rather than a continuum of
  shapes between a circle and a pill, because a control that changed its outline
  with the distance travelled would be drawing the pointer rather than the
  state; the grown knob is kept inside the capsule while it does. Let go, it
  lands on the springy curve: it passes the place it rests in by a couple of
  pixels and settles back, which is the opposite reading of the same journey
  from a family that stops where it arrives. The on colour is the family's
  semantic blue and not the theme's accent, so a switch stays that blue under
  every theme; the off track is the family's semantic grey thinned over the
  page, one alpha that answers both faces, and the knob is white on both. The
  travel rides on the normal duration, so a theme's motion scale reaches it and
  the reduce-motion switch silences it - and with it the spring, since a spring
  is a journey. There is no sentence in the control: a switch here is the
  capsule and the words beside it are a label of their own.
- `HarmonySlider`, a rail, a handle that rides it, and a bubble that says the
  number while the handle is being held. Qt's QSlider is a style's idea of a
  slider and every one of them draws a groove the toolkit owns; the drawing here
  is quiet - a rail in the family's grey with the part the handle has reached in
  its blue, and no marks under either, because a mark every interval would be a
  second answer to a question the handle has already answered. What makes it
  this family's is what happens while the pointer is down: a bubble floats over
  the handle with the value in it and fades out when the handle is let go, since
  the exact number is what the hand is asking for at that moment and nothing
  else is - a slider that printed the number, or kept the bubble there, would be
  answering after the question. The room above the rail belongs to the control
  for that one reason and is empty the rest of the time, which is why the widget
  is taller than its rail. A press anywhere on the rail moves the handle there,
  a press while held follows the pointer at once, and a step from the keyboard
  or the wheel travels on the quick duration - the same keys as every other
  family here, one convention for the project rather than one per family. The
  value is also carried to an assistive tool through a value interface.
- `HarmonyCard`, a face laid on the page and standing on a shadow. The card is
  the shape this family is built out of - a surface with a corner you can see and
  a lift under it - so the two things it carries are the family's large corner
  and one of the family's elevation levels, and the level is the whole of what
  tells two cards apart: the same face at a higher level is a card the pointer is
  on, or one that has been opened over the page. The face is the toolkit's own
  surface colour rather than the page's, so a card is a different material from
  what it lies on even where the shadow is thin, and the widget is inset by the
  room its shadow needs so the shadow is drawn beside the face rather than being
  cut off at the edge. Held, the card dims - the state a press draws is a layer
  over the surface and not a scale, because a card that shrank under the hand
  would be moving the page under the user's finger, and this family moves things
  when they arrive rather than when they are touched. The room the shadow needs
  is answered by `shadowRoom()`, so what a card carries can stay inside the face
  without the caller copying a number out of a comment.
- `HarmonyButton`, a word in a capsule at one of the three levels this family
  gives an action. `Primary` is the one action on a surface and carries the
  brand's blue at full strength with the word reversed out of it, `Secondary` is
  the action beside it standing on the family's grey thinned into the page with
  the word in the blue - a neutral capsule rather than a thinned blue one,
  because here the second level is the quiet one - and `Text` is an action that
  is only a word. The capsule is forty high with twenty either side: a control
  here is a target before it is a label, which is why it is taller than the other
  family's button. It casts nothing, because the level this family spends on a
  lift is the one a surface sits at over the page, and a button is on the page.
  Pressed, it takes the state layer rather than shrinking. Enter answers on the
  press and Space on the release, and what the button says is its accessible name
  for as long as the application has not given it a better one.

Bugfix
- The slider asks for the height its own drawing needs. The bubble floats above
  the handle and a widget cannot paint outside itself, so a height worked out
  from the handle alone drew the bubble with its top sliced off - a row or two
  on a small font, more on a large one, and a layout that squeezed the control
  towards its minimum size hint took most of the bubble away. The height is now
  computed from the caption's line together with the room the rail leaves under
  itself, and the minimum is that same height: narrower is allowed, shorter is
  not, because the bubble is not optional while the handle is held.
